      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the measure of angle \\( P \\), we need to use the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon is \\( 540^\\circ \\). The pentagon \\( ABCDE \\) is given as follows:\n\n- \\(\\angle A = 128^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle C = 113^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle D = 111^\\circ\\)\n\nWe need to find the measure of \\(\\angle P\\). First, we will find the measure of angle \\( B \\) and then use the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon is \\( 540^\\circ \\).\n\n1. Find the measure of \\(\\angle B\\):\n\\[\n\\angle B = 540^\\circ - (\\angle A + \\angle C + \\angle D + \\angle E)\n\\]\nSubstitute the given angles:\n\\[\n\\angle B = 540^\\circ - (128^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + 110^\\circ)\n\\]\nSimplify the sum inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + 110^\\circ = 462^\\circ\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\angle B = 540^\\circ - 462^\\circ = 78^\\circ\n\\]\n\n2. Now, we can find the measure of \\(\\angle P\\). Note that \\(\\angle P\\) is the angle formed by the segment \\( AE \\) and the \\( A \\)-side of the pentagon. It is the external angle to \\(\\angle ADE\\):\n\\[\n\\angle P = 180^\\circ - \\angle ADE\n\\]\nSince \\(\\angle ADE\\) is the sum of \\(\\angle D\\) and \\(\\angle E\\) minus \\(\\angle A\\):\n\\[\n\\angle ADE = \\angle D + \\angle E - \\angle A = 111^\\circ + 110^\\circ - 128^\\circ = 103^\\circ\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\angle P = 180^\\circ - 103^\\circ = 77^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle \\( P \\) is \\(\\boxed{77}\\).",
